  "slug": "differentiation-signaling-57d1",
  "summary": "Four 2026 preprints show that cytokine history, cell lineage, and viral proteins shape innate immune output in macrophages and microglia-like cells.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread across these preprints is that prior state and cell identity calibrate innate immune output rather than acute stimulation alone. In primary human macrophages, earlier cytokine exposure constrains later transcriptional responses, with IFNβ dominating. Syngeneic iPSC-derived microglia-like and CAM-like macrophages show distinct TLR-driven NF-κB responses, differing HIV permissiveness, and varied cART responses, indicating lineage-dependent behavior. In microglia-like cells, Zika virus NS2B suppresses the PA28 immunoproteasome during the innate antiviral response. A modeling study links cytokine feedback to strain-specific immune responses to avian coronavirus. What is new is the explicit dependence of these responses on signaling history and macrophage lineage, with viral proteins and feedback loops acting as modifiers of that calibration. The avian coronavirus and Zika items are less direct tests of the macrophage history and lineage question than the two macrophage studies.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
